  "image_name": "7123806e537c07399a227d6ff509ab36.png",
  "prompt_text": "The figure compares how ocean redox conditions and the nitrogen cycle changed from the Tonian through the Ediacaran. Across all panels, a coastal-to-deep-ocean cross section is shown with arrows linking N2, NH4+, NO3−, and organic nitrogen, alongside changing oxygenated versus anoxic water masses and sedimentary nitrogen isotope values.\n\nOverall, it suggests that the extent of anoxia and ice cover strongly affected which nitrogen forms dominated and how much nitrate was available. In the Tonian, shallow waters appear largely anoxic, with reduced species such as Fe2+ or H2S present and relatively low sediment δ15N values around 1–3‰. During the Cryogenian “Snowball Earth” stage, an ice sheet covers much of the ocean, and the figure contrasts very low δ15N under total ice cover with higher values in open-water settings, implying stronger nitrate-based cycling there. In the interglacial phase, surface waters become oxygenated while deeper waters remain anoxic, and nitrate production and recycling appear more developed, with δ15N rising to about 4–6‰. By the Ediacaran, oxygenated surface water persists above an anoxic deep ocean, and intermediate δ15N values of roughly 3–5‰ indicate a more stratified but still redox-sensitive marine nitrogen cycle.",
